 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Replacement Policy (2Q)
+
+The cache uses a 2Q replacement policy with three queues:
+
+- **A1in (FIFO)**: Admits newly rendered sheets that have no recent eviction history. Entries remain in A1in in insertion order; cache hits on entries in A1in do not promote them.
+- **A1out (ghost FIFO)**: Stores only cache keys (no JPEG data) of sheets recently evicted from A1in, bounded to a fixed entry capacity.
+- **Am (LRU)**: Holds promoted sheets that have been re-referenced after A1in eviction. Cache hits in Am move the entry to the most-recently-used position.
+
+On completion of a render job:
+
+1. If the key is present in **A1out**, remove it from A1out and admit the sheet into **Am**.
+2. Otherwise, admit the sheet into **A1in**.
+
+Before admitting a sheet of size $S$, while total cached JPEG bytes plus $S$ exceeds 12 GiB, evict entries:
+
+- If **A1in** exceeds its byte budget, evict the oldest entry from A1in, delete its JPEG file, and append its key to **A1out** (evicting the oldest key from A1out if A1out is at capacity).
+- Otherwise, evict the least-recently-used entry from **Am** and delete its JPEG file.
+
+The 12 GiB JPEG budget is partitioned between A1in and Am (defaulting to a 25% A1in / 75% Am split, with A1out sized to 50% of the nominal ~6,000-sheet cache capacity).
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q | LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging | Separates first-use sheets (A1in) from reused comparison sheets (Am) via a ghost queue (A1out), preventing long sequential screenings from evicting the comparison working set. LRU has lower bookkeeping and adapts faster on project switches, but sequential scans evict active comparison sheets. Frequency-based eviction with aging protects popular clips, but counter maintenance adds overhead and aging can retain stale favorites across project switches. All replacement state stays private to the preview worker. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Cache eviction policy: 2Q selected to protect comparison working sets during sequential screenings while keeping replacement state private to the worker.
+
+### Deferred
